1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,936
Meet Sam and Alex.

2
00:00:02,236 --> 00:00:07,114
They are the same age, they went to the same school, and they got the same job,

3
00:00:07,114 --> 00:00:09,707
at the same company, with the same salary.

4
00:00:10,557 --> 00:00:12,670
Fifty thousand dollars a year each.

5
00:00:12,970 --> 00:00:14,302
Not rich, not poor.

6
00:00:14,602 --> 00:00:15,577
Just normal.

7
00:00:15,877 --> 00:00:19,788
And for the first few years, their lives look almost identical.

8
00:00:20,638 --> 00:00:23,797
But thirty years later, something strange has happened.

9
00:00:24,097 --> 00:00:28,082
Alex is a millionaire, ready to retire whenever Alex wants.

10
00:00:28,382 --> 00:00:32,375
Sam is still working, still stressed, and still has credit card debt.

11
00:00:33,225 --> 00:00:34,682
Sam never got fired.

12
00:00:34,982 --> 00:00:37,024
Sam never gambled the house away.

13
00:00:37,324 --> 00:00:40,594
Sam never did anything that looked like a big mistake.

14
00:00:40,894 --> 00:00:41,983
So what happened?

15
00:00:42,833 --> 00:00:47,877
In this video, we are going to follow Sam and Alex and uncover the money trap.

16
00:00:48,177 --> 00:00:53,586
We will look at where money really goes, the silent thief that shrinks your savings,

17
00:00:53,586 --> 00:00:59,574
how debt quietly compounds against you, and how the exact same math can work for you instead.

18
00:00:59,874 --> 00:01:04,029
And at the end, I will give you a simple plan that anyone can follow.

19
00:01:04,878 --> 00:01:06,414
Quick note before we start.

20
00:01:06,714 --> 00:01:10,939
This is general education, not personal financial advice.

21
00:01:11,239 --> 00:01:13,051
Your situation is unique.

22
00:01:13,351 --> 00:01:16,862
But the math you are about to see is the same for everyone.

23
00:01:17,711 --> 00:01:18,618
Chapter one.

24
00:01:18,919 --> 00:01:20,189
Where does the money go?

25
00:01:21,389 --> 00:01:23,388
Let's start with Sam's first paycheck.

26
00:01:23,688 --> 00:01:28,731
After taxes, fifty thousand a year comes out to about thirty nine thousand.

27
00:01:29,030 --> 00:01:32,714
That's roughly three thousand two hundred and fifty dollars a month.

28
00:01:33,014 --> 00:01:34,502
It feels like a fortune.

29
00:01:35,353 --> 00:01:37,532
So Sam does what most people do.

30
00:01:37,832 --> 00:01:41,590
Sam finances a nice new car, because Sam deserves it.

31
00:01:41,890 --> 00:01:44,330
Gets the newest phone on a monthly plan.

32
00:01:44,630 --> 00:01:47,986
Grabs a fancy coffee every morning on the way to work.

33
00:01:48,286 --> 00:01:50,258
None of these feel like a big deal.

34
00:01:51,108 --> 00:01:52,246
But here's the thing.

35
00:01:52,546 --> 00:01:55,240
Sam's money works like a bucket with holes in it.

36
00:01:55,540 --> 00:02:01,033
The paycheck pours in at the top, and it leaks out the bottom through dozens of small holes.

37
00:02:01,333 --> 00:02:02,344
Subscriptions.

38
00:02:02,644 --> 00:02:03,736
Delivery apps.

39
00:02:04,037 --> 00:02:05,013
Car payments.

40
00:02:05,312 --> 00:02:06,328
Little treats.

41
00:02:06,628 --> 00:02:09,384
By the end of the month, the bucket is empty again.

42
00:02:10,234 --> 00:02:11,396
Take that coffee.

43
00:02:11,696 --> 00:02:14,234
Five dollars a day doesn't sound like much.

44
00:02:14,534 --> 00:02:20,650
But five dollars, every single day, is one thousand eight hundred and twenty five dollars a year.

45
00:02:21,500 --> 00:02:23,689
Now, this isn't a lecture about coffee.

46
00:02:23,988 --> 00:02:25,298
Enjoy your coffee.

47
00:02:25,598 --> 00:02:28,221
The real problem isn't any one purchase.

48
00:02:28,522 --> 00:02:30,327
It's that Sam has no system.

49
00:02:30,626 --> 00:02:32,316
Whatever comes in, goes out.

50
00:02:33,167 --> 00:02:35,471
And then something even sneakier happens.

51
00:02:35,771 --> 00:02:37,073
Sam gets a raise.

52
00:02:38,273 --> 00:02:41,242
Every time Sam earns more, Sam spends more.

53
00:02:41,542 --> 00:02:42,779
A bigger apartment.

54
00:02:43,079 --> 00:02:44,204
A nicer car.

55
00:02:44,504 --> 00:02:45,790
Better vacations.

56
00:02:46,091 --> 00:02:50,302
Economists call this lifestyle inflation, or lifestyle creep.

57
00:02:50,602 --> 00:02:51,631
Look at the chart.

58
00:02:51,930 --> 00:02:53,016
Income goes up.

59
00:02:53,316 --> 00:02:56,614
But spending goes up right alongside it, like a shadow.

60
00:02:56,914 --> 00:03:01,735
The gap between them, the money Sam actually keeps, stays at almost zero.

61
00:03:02,586 --> 00:03:04,932
This is what people call the rat race.

62
00:03:05,232 --> 00:03:11,639
Running faster and faster, earning more and more, and somehow ending up in exactly the same place.

63
00:03:11,940 --> 00:03:16,577
Working harder doesn't fix it, because the problem isn't how much Sam earns.

64
00:03:16,876 --> 00:03:19,152
It's what happens after the money arrives.

65
00:03:20,002 --> 00:03:21,840
Alex earns exactly the same.

66
00:03:22,140 --> 00:03:23,708
Alex enjoys life too.

67
00:03:24,009 --> 00:03:25,963
But Alex does one thing differently.

68
00:03:26,263 --> 00:03:32,842
On payday, before spending a single dollar, Alex moves a fixed amount into savings and investments.

69
00:03:33,142 --> 00:03:34,049
Automatically.

70
00:03:34,349 --> 00:03:38,627
Alex pays future Alex first, and then spends whatever is left.

71
00:03:39,478 --> 00:03:41,308
It sounds like a tiny difference.

72
00:03:41,608 --> 00:03:45,635
Sam earns, spends, and tries to save whatever is left over.

73
00:03:45,935 --> 00:03:48,162
And there is never anything left over.

74
00:03:48,462 --> 00:03:51,780
Alex earns, saves first, and then spends what's left.

75
00:03:52,080 --> 00:03:53,099
Same income.

76
00:03:53,400 --> 00:03:54,995
Completely different outcome.

77
00:03:55,295 --> 00:03:58,172
Remember this, because it will matter a lot later.

78
00:03:59,022 --> 00:03:59,996
Chapter two.

79
00:04:00,296 --> 00:04:01,442
The silent thief.

80
00:04:02,642 --> 00:04:07,360
A few years in, Sam gets a little nervous and decides to save some money.

81
00:04:07,660 --> 00:04:09,674
But Sam doesn't trust the stock market.

82
00:04:09,974 --> 00:04:10,848
Too risky.

83
00:04:11,148 --> 00:04:16,656
So Sam keeps the savings in cash, or in a bank account paying almost no interest.

84
00:04:16,956 --> 00:04:18,013
That feels safe.

85
00:04:18,313 --> 00:04:19,933
Nothing can go wrong, right?

86
00:04:20,784 --> 00:04:21,393
Wrong.

87
00:04:21,693 --> 00:04:23,795
There is a thief hiding in plain sight.

88
00:04:24,095 --> 00:04:25,481
It's called inflation.

89
00:04:25,781 --> 00:04:27,840
Every year, prices go up a little.

90
00:04:28,140 --> 00:04:32,776
The burger that cost five dollars a decade ago costs nine dollars now.

91
00:04:33,076 --> 00:04:34,113
Rent goes up.

92
00:04:34,413 --> 00:04:35,649
Groceries go up.

93
00:04:35,949 --> 00:04:37,113
Everything goes up.

94
00:04:37,963 --> 00:04:41,011
Let's say inflation averages three percent a year.

95
00:04:41,311 --> 00:04:43,020
That's a fairly normal number.

96
00:04:43,320 --> 00:04:48,760
If you keep one hundred dollars in cash, after ten years it only buys what seventy

97
00:04:48,760 --> 00:04:50,352
four dollars buys today.

98
00:04:50,651 --> 00:04:53,519
After twenty years, about fifty five dollars.

99
00:04:53,820 --> 00:04:56,466
After thirty years, just forty one dollars.

100
00:04:56,766 --> 00:04:57,800
You didn't spend it.

101
00:04:58,100 --> 00:04:59,124
You didn't lose it.

102
00:04:59,423 --> 00:05:02,351
But more than half of its value quietly disappeared.

103
00:05:03,202 --> 00:05:06,262
So cash isn't actually safe over the long run.

104
00:05:06,561 --> 00:05:09,846
It just loses value slowly enough that you don't notice.

105
00:05:10,147 --> 00:05:12,210
Sam thinks Sam is avoiding risk.

106
00:05:12,510 --> 00:05:15,468
In reality, Sam is guaranteeing a slow loss.

107
00:05:16,318 --> 00:05:18,665
Now, to be fair, you do need some cash.

108
00:05:18,965 --> 00:05:24,887
Alex keeps an emergency fund, enough to cover three to six months of essential expenses,

109
00:05:24,887 --> 00:05:27,040
in a high yield savings account.

110
00:05:27,339 --> 00:05:28,415
That's the shield.

111
00:05:28,716 --> 00:05:35,388
If Alex loses a job, or the car breaks down, there's no panic and no need for a credit card.

112
00:05:36,238 --> 00:05:41,701
But everything beyond the emergency fund, the long term money, Alex puts to work,

113
00:05:41,701 --> 00:05:46,152
in investments that have historically grown faster than inflation.

114
00:05:46,452 --> 00:05:48,709
We'll get to exactly how in a few minutes.

115
00:05:49,009 --> 00:05:52,432
First, we need to talk about the thing that's really hurting Sam.

116
00:05:53,283 --> 00:05:54,264
Chapter three.

117
00:05:54,564 --> 00:05:55,161
Debt.

118
00:05:55,461 --> 00:05:56,638
The trap closes.

119
00:05:57,838 --> 00:05:59,825
One day, Sam's car breaks down.

120
00:06:00,125 --> 00:06:02,472
The repair bill is five thousand dollars.

121
00:06:02,772 --> 00:06:04,980
Sam doesn't have an emergency fund.

122
00:06:05,280 --> 00:06:07,616
So Sam does the only thing Sam can do.

123
00:06:08,466 --> 00:06:10,434
Sam puts it on a credit card.

124
00:06:10,734 --> 00:06:13,603
The interest rate is twenty four percent a year.

125
00:06:13,903 --> 00:06:16,038
That's pretty typical for credit cards.

126
00:06:16,338 --> 00:06:18,282
Sam plans to pay it off quickly.

127
00:06:18,582 --> 00:06:21,876
Sam can afford one hundred and fifty dollars a month.

128
00:06:22,725 --> 00:06:24,203
How long does that take?

129
00:06:24,503 --> 00:06:25,795
Fifty six months.

130
00:06:26,095 --> 00:06:27,461
Almost five years.

131
00:06:28,311 --> 00:06:32,367
And in total, Sam pays about eight thousand three hundred dollars.

132
00:06:32,668 --> 00:06:36,025
More than three thousand dollars of that is pure interest.

133
00:06:36,324 --> 00:06:41,422
Sam paid for the repair, and then paid for it again, more than half over, just for

134
00:06:41,422 --> 00:06:43,100
the privilege of borrowing.

135
00:06:43,951 --> 00:06:47,417
This is the dark side of something called compound interest.

136
00:06:47,716 --> 00:06:52,672
When you owe money, interest is charged on your balance, and if you don't pay it,

137
00:06:52,672 --> 00:06:54,875
interest is charged on the interest.

138
00:06:55,175 --> 00:06:56,821
The debt grows by itself.

139
00:06:57,122 --> 00:07:01,161
It is like a ball and chain that gets heavier every month you carry it.

140
00:07:02,011 --> 00:07:03,617
And remember Sam's new car?

141
00:07:03,916 --> 00:07:06,752
Sam borrowed thirty five thousand dollars for it.

142
00:07:07,052 --> 00:07:09,768
Sam pays interest on that loan every month.

143
00:07:10,068 --> 00:07:14,227
Meanwhile, the car loses value the moment it leaves the dealership.

144
00:07:14,528 --> 00:07:18,461
After five years, it might be worth only half of what Sam paid.

145
00:07:18,760 --> 00:07:21,763
Sam is paying interest on something that is shrinking.

146
00:07:22,613 --> 00:07:25,138
Put it all together and look at Sam's paycheck.

147
00:07:25,438 --> 00:07:26,067
Rent.

148
00:07:26,367 --> 00:07:27,203
Car loan.

149
00:07:27,503 --> 00:07:28,892
Credit card payments.

150
00:07:29,192 --> 00:07:30,285
Food and fun.

151
00:07:30,586 --> 00:07:31,592
Everything else.

152
00:07:31,891 --> 00:07:32,814
And savings?

153
00:07:33,114 --> 00:07:33,812
Zero.

154
00:07:34,112 --> 00:07:39,888
A big slice of Sam's income now goes to paying for the past, instead of building the future.

155
00:07:40,738 --> 00:07:42,880
That's what consumer debt really is.

156
00:07:43,180 --> 00:07:46,454
It's your future income, spent today, plus interest.

157
00:07:46,754 --> 00:07:52,104
Every dollar of debt is a dollar that future Sam has to earn, and then hand to the bank.

158
00:07:52,954 --> 00:07:55,565
To be fair, not all debt is equally bad.

159
00:07:55,866 --> 00:08:01,569
A low interest mortgage on a home you can afford, or a loan for an education that

160
00:08:01,569 --> 00:08:04,597
clearly raises your income, can make sense.

161
00:08:04,897 --> 00:08:10,258
But high interest consumer debt, the kind used for stuff that loses value, is the

162
00:08:10,258 --> 00:08:13,369
single biggest wealth killer for normal people.

163
00:08:14,219 --> 00:08:16,074
Alex has a few simple rules.

164
00:08:16,374 --> 00:08:20,992
Pay the credit card in full, every month, so interest is never charged.

165
00:08:21,292 --> 00:08:24,868
Use the emergency fund, not debt, for surprises.

166
00:08:25,168 --> 00:08:28,971
Buy reliable used cars and drive them for a long time.

167
00:08:29,270 --> 00:08:33,558
And if there ever is high interest debt, pay it off before anything else.

168
00:08:33,858 --> 00:08:39,083
Because paying off a twenty four percent credit card is like earning a guaranteed

169
00:08:39,083 --> 00:08:40,825
twenty four percent return.

170
00:08:41,675 --> 00:08:42,659
Chapter four.

171
00:08:42,958 --> 00:08:44,672
The eighth wonder of the world.

172
00:08:45,872 --> 00:08:51,759
There's a famous quote, often attributed to Albert Einstein, though nobody can prove he said it.

173
00:08:52,059 --> 00:08:55,267
Compound interest is the eighth wonder of the world.

174
00:08:55,567 --> 00:08:57,296
He who understands it, earns it.

175
00:08:57,595 --> 00:08:58,838
He who doesn't, pays it.

176
00:08:59,139 --> 00:09:00,556
Sam has been paying it.

177
00:09:00,855 --> 00:09:03,232
Now let's see what happens when Alex earns it.

178
00:09:04,082 --> 00:09:06,410
Think of a snowball rolling down a hill.

179
00:09:06,710 --> 00:09:08,028
At first it's tiny.

180
00:09:08,328 --> 00:09:10,475
It picks up a little snow on each turn.

181
00:09:10,776 --> 00:09:14,191
But the bigger it gets, the more snow it picks up per turn.

182
00:09:14,490 --> 00:09:16,124
Growth creates more growth.

183
00:09:16,424 --> 00:09:17,595
That's compounding.

184
00:09:17,896 --> 00:09:22,662
Your money earns a return, and then that return starts earning its own return.

185
00:09:23,512 --> 00:09:24,911
Here's Alex's plan.

186
00:09:25,210 --> 00:09:29,735
Starting at age twenty five, Alex invests three hundred dollars a month.

187
00:09:30,035 --> 00:09:30,817
That's it.

188
00:09:31,118 --> 00:09:32,159
Not a fortune.

189
00:09:32,459 --> 00:09:33,976
About ten dollars a day.

190
00:09:34,276 --> 00:09:37,496
And let's assume an average return of eight percent a year.

191
00:09:37,796 --> 00:09:42,640
That's an assumption, not a promise, but it's in the range of what a broad stock

192
00:09:42,640 --> 00:09:45,909
market fund has returned over long periods of history.

193
00:09:46,759 --> 00:09:47,985
Watch what happens.

194
00:09:48,284 --> 00:09:50,896
For the first ten years, it's honestly boring.

195
00:09:51,197 --> 00:09:56,798
Alex puts in thirty six thousand dollars, and it grows to about fifty five thousand.

196
00:09:57,098 --> 00:09:58,601
Nice, but nothing special.

197
00:09:58,901 --> 00:10:03,067
After twenty years, about one hundred and seventy seven thousand.

198
00:10:03,366 --> 00:10:05,021
Then the curve starts to bend.

199
00:10:05,321 --> 00:10:08,966
After thirty years, four hundred and forty seven thousand.

200
00:10:09,266 --> 00:10:13,764
And after forty years, at age sixty five, over one million dollars.

201
00:10:14,614 --> 00:10:16,267
Here's the incredible part.

202
00:10:16,566 --> 00:10:21,590
Alex only put in one hundred and forty four thousand dollars of actual money.

203
00:10:21,890 --> 00:10:25,487
The other nine hundred thousand dollars came from compounding.

204
00:10:25,787 --> 00:10:27,699
From returns earning returns.

205
00:10:27,999 --> 00:10:29,814
The money did most of the work.

206
00:10:30,664 --> 00:10:33,519
There's a handy shortcut called the rule of seventy two.

207
00:10:33,819 --> 00:10:38,628
Divide seventy two by your annual return, and you get roughly how many years it

208
00:10:38,628 --> 00:10:40,271
takes your money to double.

209
00:10:40,571 --> 00:10:44,251
At eight percent, seventy two divided by eight is nine.

210
00:10:44,550 --> 00:10:47,382
Your money roughly doubles every nine years.

211
00:10:47,682 --> 00:10:50,608
Over forty years, that's more than four doublings.

212
00:10:51,459 --> 00:10:54,565
Now here's the most important part of this whole video.

213
00:10:54,865 --> 00:10:57,951
The secret ingredient of compounding isn't money.

214
00:10:58,251 --> 00:10:59,099
It's time.

215
00:11:00,298 --> 00:11:01,882
Imagine a different story.

216
00:11:02,182 --> 00:11:07,691
Alex invests three hundred a month from twenty five to thirty five, just ten years,

217
00:11:07,691 --> 00:11:13,798
thirty six thousand dollars total, and then stops completely, and never adds another dollar.

218
00:11:14,098 --> 00:11:19,016
Sam wakes up at thirty five and invests three hundred a month for thirty straight

219
00:11:19,016 --> 00:11:22,052
years, a hundred and eight thousand dollars total.

220
00:11:22,352 --> 00:11:24,042
Who has more at sixty five?

221
00:11:24,892 --> 00:11:25,549
Alex.

222
00:11:25,849 --> 00:11:31,524
With about six hundred thousand dollars, versus Sam's four hundred and forty seven thousand.

223
00:11:31,824 --> 00:11:37,142
Alex put in one third of the money, and still ended up with more, simply by starting

224
00:11:37,142 --> 00:11:38,281
ten years earlier.

225
00:11:38,581 --> 00:11:41,535
Those early dollars had more time to snowball.

226
00:11:42,385 --> 00:11:45,707
So if you take one thing from this video, let it be this.

227
00:11:46,007 --> 00:11:48,254
The best time to start was years ago.

228
00:11:48,554 --> 00:11:50,287
The second best time is today.

229
00:11:50,587 --> 00:11:51,843
Not when you earn more.

230
00:11:52,143 --> 00:11:53,712
Not when things calm down.

231
00:11:54,011 --> 00:11:56,079
Today, even if it's a small amount.

232
00:11:56,930 --> 00:11:57,974
Chapter five.

233
00:11:58,274 --> 00:12:00,162
Assets versus liabilities.

234
00:12:01,362 --> 00:12:04,279
There's a simple way to think about everything you own.

235
00:12:04,579 --> 00:12:06,720
An asset puts money into your pocket.

236
00:12:07,021 --> 00:12:09,553
A liability takes money out of your pocket.

237
00:12:09,853 --> 00:12:13,983
A car loan, a big TV on credit, a pile of subscriptions.

238
00:12:14,282 --> 00:12:15,872
Those are liabilities.

239
00:12:16,172 --> 00:12:19,623
Investments, a business, or skills that raise your income.

240
00:12:19,923 --> 00:12:21,125
Those are assets.

241
00:12:21,975 --> 00:12:23,042
Here's the twist.

242
00:12:23,342 --> 00:12:25,437
Sam often looks richer than Alex.

243
00:12:25,737 --> 00:12:29,481
Nicer car, newer clothes, more expensive vacations.

244
00:12:29,781 --> 00:12:32,467
But looking rich and being rich are opposites.

245
00:12:32,767 --> 00:12:37,519
Every dollar Sam spends to look rich is a dollar that will never compound.

246
00:12:37,819 --> 00:12:42,814
Alex looks perfectly normal, while a money tree quietly grows in the background.

247
00:12:43,665 --> 00:12:45,497
Wealth is mostly invisible.

248
00:12:45,796 --> 00:12:50,367
It's the car that wasn't bought, the upgrade that was skipped, the money that is

249
00:12:50,367 --> 00:12:52,709
still sitting in an account, compounding.

250
00:12:53,009 --> 00:12:56,468
You can't see someone's net worth by looking at their driveway.

251
00:12:57,318 --> 00:13:00,675
And early in life, your biggest asset is actually you.

252
00:13:00,975 --> 00:13:02,252
Your ability to earn.

253
00:13:02,552 --> 00:13:07,940
Alex spends some money on courses, certifications, and learning new skills, and

254
00:13:07,940 --> 00:13:11,623
over the years, that turns into promotions and raises.

255
00:13:11,923 --> 00:13:16,200
Investing in yourself is one of the few investments with no ceiling.

256
00:13:17,050 --> 00:13:18,192
And here's the key.

257
00:13:18,492 --> 00:13:23,714
When Alex gets a raise, Alex doesn't let lifestyle inflation swallow all of it.

258
00:13:24,014 --> 00:13:28,528
A simple rule: at least half of every raise goes straight to investments.

259
00:13:28,828 --> 00:13:33,668
Alex still gets to enjoy life a little more each year, but the gap between earning

260
00:13:33,668 --> 00:13:35,262
and spending keeps growing.

261
00:13:35,562 --> 00:13:37,510
That gap is the engine of wealth.

262
00:13:38,360 --> 00:13:39,331
Chapter six.

263
00:13:39,631 --> 00:13:41,397
Where should the money actually go?

264
00:13:42,597 --> 00:13:44,706
Sam finally decides to invest.

265
00:13:45,006 --> 00:13:50,296
Sam hears a hot tip from a friend, buys a single stock that everyone is talking

266
00:13:50,296 --> 00:13:53,979
about, and watches it drop forty percent in two months.

267
00:13:54,279 --> 00:13:58,519
Sam panics, sells, and decides investing is basically gambling.

268
00:13:59,369 --> 00:14:01,447
Alex does something much more boring.

269
00:14:01,748 --> 00:14:05,642
Instead of trying to pick the winners, Alex buys the whole basket.

270
00:14:05,942 --> 00:14:11,854
A broad, low cost index fund holds hundreds or even thousands of companies at once.

271
00:14:12,154 --> 00:14:14,271
When some go down, others go up.

272
00:14:14,571 --> 00:14:16,538
Alex doesn't need to be a genius.

273
00:14:16,838 --> 00:14:19,981
Alex just owns a small piece of the whole economy.

274
00:14:20,831 --> 00:14:22,274
Does the market go down?

275
00:14:22,574 --> 00:14:23,531
Absolutely.

276
00:14:23,831 --> 00:14:25,041
Sometimes a lot.

277
00:14:25,341 --> 00:14:28,417
Crashes and recessions happen every few years.

278
00:14:28,717 --> 00:14:34,489
But historically, over long periods, broad markets have gone up far more than they've gone down.

279
00:14:34,789 --> 00:14:38,115
The ride is bumpy, but the direction has been upward.

280
00:14:38,415 --> 00:14:43,484
The people who got hurt the most were usually the ones who panicked and sold at the bottom.

281
00:14:44,334 --> 00:14:46,692
Alex also pays attention to fees.

282
00:14:46,992 --> 00:14:48,925
A one percent fee sounds tiny.

283
00:14:49,225 --> 00:14:52,208
But take five hundred dollars a month for forty years.

284
00:14:52,508 --> 00:14:56,376
At seven percent, you'd end up with about one point three million.

285
00:14:56,676 --> 00:15:01,952
With a one percent annual fee eating into that return, you'd end up with under one million.

286
00:15:02,252 --> 00:15:06,248
That small fee quietly costs over three hundred thousand dollars.

287
00:15:07,098 --> 00:15:09,584
And finally, Alex makes it automatic.

288
00:15:09,885 --> 00:15:15,974
On payday, money moves into the investment account without Alex having to decide anything.

289
00:15:16,274 --> 00:15:17,648
No willpower needed.

290
00:15:17,948 --> 00:15:19,331
No checking the news.

291
00:15:19,631 --> 00:15:21,738
No trying to guess the perfect moment.

292
00:15:22,037 --> 00:15:25,663
Alex basically forgets about it and lets time do its thing.

293
00:15:26,513 --> 00:15:32,378
If your employer offers a retirement plan, especially one with a matching contribution,

294
00:15:32,378 --> 00:15:34,738
that match is basically free money.

295
00:15:35,038 --> 00:15:35,709
Take it.

296
00:15:36,010 --> 00:15:40,662
Many countries also have tax advantaged accounts for long term investing.

297
00:15:40,962 --> 00:15:46,138
The exact rules depend on where you live, so it's worth spending an afternoon learning

298
00:15:46,138 --> 00:15:47,582
what's available to you.

299
00:15:48,432 --> 00:15:53,187
Before we get to the plan, let's deal with the three excuses that keep most people

300
00:15:53,187 --> 00:15:54,231
stuck in the trap.

301
00:15:54,531 --> 00:15:56,165
Sam has used all three.

302
00:15:57,365 --> 00:15:58,593
Excuse number one.

303
00:15:58,893 --> 00:16:00,443
I don't earn enough to invest.

304
00:16:00,743 --> 00:16:03,765
It's true that a bigger income makes everything easier.

305
00:16:04,065 --> 00:16:06,284
But the habit matters more than the amount.

306
00:16:06,584 --> 00:16:09,037
Fifty dollars a month is a real start.

307
00:16:09,337 --> 00:16:14,651
It builds the system, and when the income grows, the system is already there, waiting.

308
00:16:14,951 --> 00:16:18,952
Plenty of high earners are broke, because the habit never formed.

309
00:16:19,802 --> 00:16:21,121
Excuse number two.

310
00:16:21,421 --> 00:16:22,525
I'll start later.

311
00:16:22,825 --> 00:16:24,137
After the next raise.

312
00:16:24,437 --> 00:16:25,464
After the wedding.

313
00:16:25,763 --> 00:16:27,230
After things calm down.

314
00:16:27,530 --> 00:16:29,119
But things never calm down.

315
00:16:29,419 --> 00:16:34,954
And as we saw, every year you wait costs you the most powerful years of compounding,

316
00:16:34,954 --> 00:16:38,644
the ones at the very end, where the snowball is biggest.

317
00:16:38,945 --> 00:16:42,887
Waiting ten years can cut your final result by more than half.

318
00:16:43,737 --> 00:16:45,094
Excuse number three.

319
00:16:45,394 --> 00:16:48,124
I'll wait for the market to drop, and then buy.

320
00:16:48,424 --> 00:16:49,489
It sounds smart.

321
00:16:49,790 --> 00:16:54,160
But even professional investors struggle to time the market consistently.

322
00:16:54,460 --> 00:17:00,366
While Sam waits for the perfect moment, the market often climbs higher, and Sam waits some more.

323
00:17:00,666 --> 00:17:02,384
Alex has a simpler approach.

324
00:17:02,684 --> 00:17:06,364
Invest the same amount every month, whatever the news says.

325
00:17:06,665 --> 00:17:09,497
When prices are low, that money buys more.

326
00:17:09,797 --> 00:17:12,177
When prices are high, it buys less.

327
00:17:12,477 --> 00:17:14,360
Over time, it averages out.

328
00:17:14,659 --> 00:17:16,802
This is called dollar cost averaging.

329
00:17:17,652 --> 00:17:19,875
There's an old saying among investors.

330
00:17:20,175 --> 00:17:22,481
Time in the market beats timing the market.

331
00:17:22,781 --> 00:17:24,706
Consistency beats cleverness.

332
00:17:25,006 --> 00:17:26,796
Alex isn't smarter than Sam.

333
00:17:27,097 --> 00:17:28,880
Alex is just more consistent.

334
00:17:29,730 --> 00:17:34,265
And honestly, the biggest risk for most investors isn't the market at all.

335
00:17:34,565 --> 00:17:36,057
It's their own emotions.

336
00:17:36,357 --> 00:17:38,898
Fear makes people sell when prices crash.

337
00:17:39,198 --> 00:17:42,443
Greed makes people chase whatever went up last year.

338
00:17:42,743 --> 00:17:48,031
The best investors are often the ones who set up a sensible plan, automate it, and

339
00:17:48,031 --> 00:17:50,223
then stop looking at it every day.

340
00:17:51,073 --> 00:17:52,070
Chapter seven.

341
00:17:52,370 --> 00:17:53,486
The simple plan.

342
00:17:54,686 --> 00:17:55,911
So what about Sam?

343
00:17:56,211 --> 00:17:57,234
Is it too late?

344
00:17:57,534 --> 00:17:58,071
No.

345
00:17:58,371 --> 00:18:01,764
It is never too late to stop the leak and start compounding.

346
00:18:02,064 --> 00:18:03,656
Sam just needs a plan.

347
00:18:03,956 --> 00:18:05,658
And here it is, step by step.

348
00:18:06,508 --> 00:18:07,323
Step one.

349
00:18:07,623 --> 00:18:09,341
Track your spending for one month.

350
00:18:09,641 --> 00:18:10,500
Just look.

351
00:18:10,800 --> 00:18:12,278
Find the holes in the bucket.

352
00:18:12,579 --> 00:18:13,452
Step two.

353
00:18:13,752 --> 00:18:19,470
Build a small starter emergency fund, even one thousand dollars, so the next surprise

354
00:18:19,470 --> 00:18:21,353
doesn't go on a credit card.

355
00:18:21,652 --> 00:18:22,579
Step three.

356
00:18:22,880 --> 00:18:26,102
Attack high interest debt, starting with the highest rate.

357
00:18:26,401 --> 00:18:27,307
Step four.

358
00:18:27,607 --> 00:18:28,865
Pay yourself first.

359
00:18:29,165 --> 00:18:31,761
Set up an automatic transfer on payday.

360
00:18:32,061 --> 00:18:32,984
Step five.

361
00:18:33,284 --> 00:18:38,296
Invest for the long term in broad, low cost index funds, and leave them alone.

362
00:18:38,595 --> 00:18:39,501
Step six.

363
00:18:39,801 --> 00:18:43,021
Every time you get a raise, save at least half of it.

364
00:18:43,871 --> 00:18:48,148
If you want a simple starting point, try the fifty thirty twenty rule.

365
00:18:48,448 --> 00:18:53,576
Fifty percent of your take home pay for needs, like housing, food, and transport.

366
00:18:53,876 --> 00:18:56,206
Thirty percent for wants, the fun stuff.

367
00:18:56,506 --> 00:18:59,338
And twenty percent for savings and paying off debt.

368
00:18:59,637 --> 00:19:03,386
It's not perfect for everyone, but it's a great first framework.

369
00:19:04,237 --> 00:19:05,331
And Sam starts.

370
00:19:05,631 --> 00:19:06,710
Small at first.

371
00:19:07,009 --> 00:19:10,131
Cancels a few subscriptions nobody was using.

372
00:19:10,431 --> 00:19:11,934
Pays off the credit card.

373
00:19:12,234 --> 00:19:16,696
Starts investing two hundred dollars a month, then three hundred, then more.

374
00:19:16,996 --> 00:19:18,226
It's not dramatic.

375
00:19:18,526 --> 00:19:19,721
It's not exciting.

376
00:19:20,020 --> 00:19:21,978
But the snowball has started rolling.

377
00:19:22,828 --> 00:19:26,516
Because in the end, the money trap isn't about how much you earn.

378
00:19:26,816 --> 00:19:28,613
It's about three simple things.

379
00:19:28,913 --> 00:19:30,348
Spend less than you earn.

380
00:19:30,648 --> 00:19:31,957
Invest the difference.

381
00:19:32,257 --> 00:19:33,376
And give it time.

382
00:19:33,676 --> 00:19:34,458
That's it.

383
00:19:34,758 --> 00:19:36,010
That's the whole secret.

384
00:19:36,310 --> 00:19:37,156
It's simple.

385
00:19:37,456 --> 00:19:38,746
It's just not easy.

386
00:19:39,596 --> 00:19:42,628
Sam and Alex will be back with more money lessons.

387
00:19:42,928 --> 00:19:47,442
If this video helped you, subscribe, and share it with a friend who keeps saying

388
00:19:47,442 --> 00:19:49,248
they'll start saving next month.

389
00:19:49,548 --> 00:19:52,246
Thanks for watching, and see you in the next one.
